#X obj 2100 420 loadbang;
#X obj 2100 470 t b b;
#X msg 2200 520 init;
#X obj 2100 1000 spigot 1;
#X msg 2250 950 0;
#X msg 2100 1050 fail u_song no-lua;
#X obj 2100 1100 s err;
#X obj 2400 420 declare -lib pdlua;
#X text 20 1200 The rules live in u_song.lua: the sections \, which one plays and which is queued \, the top row \, the bars \, the draft against the saved song and the files' text. u_song-lua is its Pd face \, and reads and writes the files. Edit the rules there \, not here: test/gate/lua/u_song_test.lua runs them without Pd. A message it does not know is fail u_song bad-selector \, a Lua error fail u_song lua-<selector> \, and a file it can't write fail u_song write-failed (C-12)., f 50;
#X text 1200 700 The section policy is this file's: u_state routes put auto and put manual and drops the rest \, so put section reaches only here. A part puts back what it restores (ref/module/state.md) \, and that put lands in the section now playing \, unchanged \, so a section starting leaves nothing unsaved., f 40;
#X text 900 700 Load \, then arm the draft's timer: t b b fires right to left. The draft is written every 2 s when it has changed \, and never before the load has read it \, which would write over the last session with this one's defaults (ref/module/state.md)., f 22;
#X text 1550 520 PLAY counts the section's bars from the pattern's step 1. STOP and panic start a section queued at once: no bar is coming., f 30;
#X text 2100 1200 At load the Lua answers init with a bang on its fifth outlet \, which shuts the spigot. No bang means it never loaded -- pdlua missing \, or an error in the Lua -- and fail u_song no-lua says so on the screen: the top row \, save and revert do nothing., f 40;
